00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:51.742
Everyone, I'm pretty sure, is
using some kind of open source.

00:03:51.742 --> 00:03:52.700
And that's really cool.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:55.270
A lot of features come in
and it reflects users voices

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:56.390
very quickly.

00:03:56.390 --> 00:03:58.690
But when it comes
down to actually using

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:00.660
production, using
it on your own,

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:02.500
it is actually your
responsibility.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:04.810
You have to meet--
mostly security team

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:10.950
has to actually be in charge
of assuring its integrity.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:13.470
Also on top of that,
the vulnerabilities

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:18.870
around containers keep coming
up, which I'll dig deeper.

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:23.160
Most recently, there was a
runC container escape in runC.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:25.500
How many people
heard about this?

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:26.013
Oh, yeah.

00:04:26.013 --> 00:04:28.180
This one's, actually, kind
of [? Wiley ?] mentioned.

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:29.720
So it's pretty understandable.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:32.710
So just to explain
a little bit more--

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:35.280
runC is the engine of
the container runtime,

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:39.450
like Docker container,
Containerd, and cri-o.

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:43.290
And in this case, if
the attacker's process

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:46.110
was running as root
inside a container,

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:50.310
the attacker was able to gain
the root access of the host

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:53.940
by overriding your runC
binary from inside.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:56.310
An obvious way to
mitigate this threat

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:59.400
was to run the container
as a non-root, of course.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:01.320
And apparently,
this is definitely

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:03.990
the recommended way of
running your containers

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:05.620
in the first place.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:09.210
However, now, even if you
did run your containers

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:12.660
as non-root, you still need
to think about the container

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:14.860
escape through the Linux kernel.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:16.330
So not many people
know about this,

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:18.310
but the Linux kernel has
a huge attack surface.

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:20.400
And this type of
container escape

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:24.510
can happen by exploiting the
bugs inside the Linux kernel.

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:27.000
And if the attack
was successful,

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:31.500
the attacker may be able to
bypass the container mechanism

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:36.650
or elevate its privilege.

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:41.540
One of the most well-known
cases of this is Dirty COW.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:46.310
This is a privilege escalation
using a bug in the Linux kernel

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:49.670
by exploiting a race condition
in the copy-on-write system,

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:51.940
COW, in the memory system--

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:53.420
in the memory subsystem.

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:57.410
The non-local user was able
to increase their privileges

00:05:57.410 --> 00:05:58.860
on the system.

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:02.270
So again, containers
do not contain.

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:06.770
Thus, you would definitely want
to think about running only

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:08.720
trusted code in your system.

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:19.840
Now, if your system were
running trusted code,

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:23.070
you're following the right best
practice and lower privilege,

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:24.170
that's really great.

00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:29.090
However, in case of
SaaS and multi-tenancy,

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:32.960
things don't necessarily
work that way.

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:36.640
If you're running a SaaS,
like Tim, my customer,

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:39.460
your customers will be--

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:44.260
would like to upload their code
to run their favorite plugins

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:45.490
on your service.

00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:49.330
Like in case of, for example,
in our Google Service,

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:52.450
like Cloud Run could be a
good example that we launch.

00:06:52.450 --> 00:06:56.950
If you have your functions or
code in service containers,

00:06:56.950 --> 00:06:59.320
your cluster users will
be uploading whatever code

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:01.670
they want to to your system.

00:07:01.670 --> 00:07:04.270
And you, as a service
provider, want

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:08.080
to share a cluster
to save the cost.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:11.590
As a consequence,
basically, an untrusted code

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:16.270
can be running on the
shared cluster by default.

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:18.290
Now, therefore, we
need a solution.

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:19.290
And there's a good news.

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:25.110
There are containers
actually do contain,

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:28.130
and they are called sandbox.

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:31.100
Sandbox is a hot
topic in Kubernetes.

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:35.480
In fact, there are a number of
sandbox technologies emerged

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:40.310
in open source in just
the last year and a half.

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:42.180
And also, in the
Kubernetes world,

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:44.930
Kubernetes Runtime
Class was added

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:51.320
to provide an API to access to
this to sandbox your containers

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:53.030
together with the new
technologies that's

00:07:53.030 --> 00:07:56.330
happening in open source world.

00:07:56.330 --> 00:07:58.940
Now, open source
technologies are coming up.

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:03.790
And now the question is, do
we have the product for that?

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:07.990
With that, we're very excited
to introduce the GKE Sandbox

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:10.390
on Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:13.870
As the name says,
it provides sandbox,

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:19.110
containers do contain, to
GKE as defense-in-depth

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:21.470
at the pod level.

00:08:21.470 --> 00:08:24.650
It is based on
open source gVisor,

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:28.190
which is what Google invented.

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:31.730
And then also widely used
in the Google internally.

00:08:31.730 --> 00:08:34.880
Now, since we open
sourced gVisor,

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:37.850
you might have heard about
Google products that's

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:38.750
using gVisor.

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:41.270
The most recent one
is the Cloud Run

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:45.060
that we announced at Next, at
the keynote on the first day.

00:08:49.340 --> 00:08:57.250
And one of the reasons that
gVisor, powering GKE Sandbox,

00:08:57.250 --> 00:09:01.660
got so popular at Google
is that it does not require

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:03.490
any application changes.

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:06.460
If you have your workloads,
you have your containers,

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:08.260
you don't necessarily
have to change

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:10.300
to get your stuff sandboxed.

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:12.430
So if you're thinking
about how to use this,

00:09:12.430 --> 00:09:14.380
you don't have to
actually think about it.

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:16.180
You just have to
turn it on and then

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:17.755
run your pods on GKE Sandbox.

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:24.250
So how can GKE Sandbox help you?

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:26.660
GKE Sandbox can help you by--

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:28.210
it's really easy to use.

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:31.160
It has defense-in-depth
without hassling.

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:33.200
First of all, it's part of GKE.

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:37.580
The Google Kubernetes Engine is
a managed Kubernetes service.

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:41.120
This GKE Sandbox is fully
integrated into GKE.

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:43.220
Thus, you don't have
to install anything.

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:46.100
You don't have to install
open source gVisor.

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:47.870
We already baked in everything.

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:50.167
And we made a toggle in
the switch very simple

00:09:50.167 --> 00:09:51.500
that we're going to talk deeper.

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:54.420
So if you have a container,
if you're already on GKE,

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:57.290
you just couple switch,
and there you go.

00:09:57.290 --> 00:09:59.330
In addition to that, I
just kind of mentioned,

00:09:59.330 --> 00:10:02.690
the technology is behind
the scene is gVisor.

00:10:02.690 --> 00:10:05.120
And like I mentioned,
you don't have

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:06.550
to change your application.

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:08.300
You don't have to
change your applications

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:11.180
or not know a new
mental model to use it.

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:13.970
You just run it as if
you run your regular--

00:10:13.970 --> 00:10:17.050
your pods regularly.

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:20.380
Second, it limits
the potential attacks

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:23.860
by reducing attack surface.

00:10:23.860 --> 00:10:26.710
Today, the container and
the host operating system

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:28.630
are tied together.

00:10:28.630 --> 00:10:31.960
What GKE Sandbox
provides are two layers

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:34.510
of isolation in between.

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:35.050
Like this.

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:36.630
I can't actually hold it.

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:41.280
But the first layer of isolation
is the user [? line ?] kernel

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:44.230
written in Go that
gVisor provides.

00:10:44.230 --> 00:10:46.930
And then even if that
gets compromised,

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:49.800
there is additional filter,
seccomp and namespace,

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:51.670
that would actually
prevent further attack.

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:55.930
So from here, sitting together,
to two layers in between.

00:10:55.930 --> 00:10:59.560
We're going to dig deeper
on this one as well.

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:02.250
Third, GKE Sandbox
can help teams

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:05.280
who want to run SaaS
and multi-tenancy,

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:08.980
because it provides the
strong isolation boundaries.

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:11.550
For example, like Cloud Run
that got announced again,

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:15.060
is using gVisor, the same
technology behind GKE Sandbox.

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:18.330
When you have such untrusted
code coming here and there

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:21.030
all the time, you
do need isolation.

00:11:21.030 --> 00:11:23.790
Therefore, you want to think
about this kind of technology

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:27.760
sandbox to be used
in your system.

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:30.720
So I think GKE Sandbox should
be a great fit for those

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:33.510
who are planning to
go in multi-tenancy

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:36.430
or who have SaaS
similar to Cloud

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:38.030
Run, and many further services.

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:44.070
With that, I'm going
to hand it over to Ian

00:11:44.070 --> 00:11:47.290
to talk more about the
technology backing up

00:11:47.290 --> 00:11:51.710
GKE Sandbox, which is gVisor
that is already in open source.

00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:52.590
Ian.

00:11:52.590 --> 00:11:55.132
IAN LEWIS: OK, so I'm going to
be talking about gVisor, which

00:11:55.132 --> 00:12:00.120
is the open source
project that is

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:06.490
what is used behind the
scenes by GKE Sandbox.

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:09.850
So gVisor is an open source
project, container sandbox

00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:10.990
runtime.

00:12:10.990 --> 00:12:20.890
And what that is is it's an
OCI runtime, much like runC,

00:12:20.890 --> 00:12:22.550
if you're familiar with that.

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:25.390
It's used extensively at Google
for a number of different use

00:12:25.390 --> 00:12:26.750
cases.

00:12:26.750 --> 00:12:28.870
But as Yoshi
mentioned, for Cloud,

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:35.350
it's used for certain products
that run untrusted code,

00:12:35.350 --> 00:12:41.430
for things like Cloud Run, and
our new App Engine runtimes.

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:47.860
As an open source project,
it's available on GitHub.

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:50.020
And we have a lot
of documentation

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:52.330
available at gVisor.dev.

00:12:52.330 --> 00:12:54.520
So if you're interested
in the project,

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:56.410
definitely check
out the website.

00:12:56.410 --> 00:13:00.880
We have lots of documentation on
the architecture, the security

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:03.340
model, and lots more.

00:13:03.340 --> 00:13:04.840
And we'll also be--

00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:09.603
we have mailing lists and a
community meeting next week.

00:13:09.603 --> 00:13:11.020
So if you're
interested in getting

00:13:11.020 --> 00:13:13.930
involved with the project,
definitely checking it out.

00:13:13.930 --> 00:13:16.390
All of that information
is on the community page

00:13:16.390 --> 00:13:17.040
on the website.

00:13:19.830 --> 00:13:22.560
So to give some
background into gVisor,

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:27.480
and why we developed it,
and the thinking behind it,

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:30.870
we wanted to be able to run
untrusted code on behalf

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:33.570
of users.

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:36.060
But at Google, we
use containers a lot

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:37.755
as part of our infrastructure.

00:13:37.755 --> 00:13:40.380
And typically, the way that you
would build a sandbox like this

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:42.420
is you would use some
sort of hardware isolation

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:48.030
mechanism, like a VM, or
a Virtual Machine Manager.

00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:50.220
But that doesn't
really necessarily fit

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:52.350
with our architecture
in a way that we liked.

00:13:52.350 --> 00:13:56.790
And we wanted it to be
much more container-like,

00:13:56.790 --> 00:14:01.260
and for it to be something
that really was very resource

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:03.310
efficient at scale.

00:14:03.310 --> 00:14:05.760
So we obviously need
something that provides us

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:11.490
that defense-in-depth, that
extra layer of isolation,

00:14:11.490 --> 00:14:15.172
but gave us a lightweight
way of running a container.

00:14:15.172 --> 00:14:17.130
We wanted something that
was very low overhead,

00:14:17.130 --> 00:14:19.410
something that was able to
start and stop containers

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:21.600
very quickly and easily.

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:26.980
But we also wanted something
that was zero configuration.

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:30.780
So if you're not using
a VMM, then usually you

00:14:30.780 --> 00:14:35.370
would do something like using
AppArmor or seccomp, which

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:39.540
tend to be based on
configuration or filtering,

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:42.720
and they depend a lot on
how the application behaves.

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:44.880
So you typically
filter out the things

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:48.130
that the application
doesn't need to do.

00:14:48.130 --> 00:14:50.520
But you need to manage that
configuration over time

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:52.320
as the application changes.

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:55.170
And it's very important
from a security standpoint

00:14:55.170 --> 00:14:58.530
that you keep that
configuration correct.

00:14:58.530 --> 00:15:01.410
Like if your application
starts doing less things,

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:04.740
you need to then update it so
that the filter includes those

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:07.980
things and it doesn't--

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:10.710
it's very hard from a security
standpoint to make sure

00:15:10.710 --> 00:15:14.270
that all of the applications
that you're deploying

00:15:14.270 --> 00:15:17.460
are managed correctly.

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:20.490
And we also wanted to be able
to run binaries unmodified.

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:24.240
We didn't want to have to
update our applications

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:26.813
with a new library,
or link to a new thing

00:15:26.813 --> 00:15:28.480
in order to get the
applications to run.

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:32.910
We wanted to just be able to
run regular applications that

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:34.920
were built for Linux.

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:42.860
So we built gVisor in order
to solve those problems.

00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:47.090
And what gVisor does is
it provides a user space

00:15:47.090 --> 00:15:53.540
kernel that is run in user
space with your container.

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:57.860
And as it runs your container,
or your container application,

00:15:57.860 --> 00:16:01.570
it intercepts the system calls
that your application makes

00:16:01.570 --> 00:16:04.470
and handles those in user space.

00:16:04.470 --> 00:16:06.470
So in that sense, it's
very similar to something

00:16:06.470 --> 00:16:11.120
like user space Linux, but
it's a totally new kernel

00:16:11.120 --> 00:16:13.960
that's implemented in
Go using the Go runtime.

00:16:13.960 --> 00:16:20.600
And we chose Go because
it's a very good runtime.

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:24.650
It provides a lot of things,
like garbage collection,

00:16:24.650 --> 00:16:29.000
as well as type
safety, so things

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:34.520
that they can help with the
security of the user space

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:35.240
kernel itself.

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:40.730
And each pod is isolated with
its own user space kernel.

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:42.230
So we don't have a
single user space

00:16:42.230 --> 00:16:43.535
kernel for all containers.

00:16:43.535 --> 00:16:45.410
And this keeps all of
the containers isolated

00:16:45.410 --> 00:16:46.688
from each other.

00:16:46.688 --> 00:16:48.230
But by having this
user space kernel,

00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:49.970
we're also isolating
from the host,

00:16:49.970 --> 00:16:54.000
so that the host interaction
is greatly reduced.

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:57.070
And so we don't have containers
interacting with the host

00:16:57.070 --> 00:16:59.540
and able to get host
level privileges.

00:17:03.954 --> 00:17:06.079
So diving a little bit
deeper into the architecture

00:17:06.079 --> 00:17:12.150
for gVisor, gVisor provides
an OCI runtime interface.

00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:15.589
So this is-- the OCI is the
Open Containers Initiative.

00:17:15.589 --> 00:17:19.740
And this is a standard
for running containers.

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:22.579
And so we provide
an OCI interface

00:17:22.579 --> 00:17:25.569
in the form of an
application called runsc.

00:17:25.569 --> 00:17:29.510
And this is a single binary that
you can use to deploy gVisor

00:17:29.510 --> 00:17:32.230
and install it.

00:17:32.230 --> 00:17:35.020
And so it's basically
a drop-in replacement

00:17:35.020 --> 00:17:39.480
for the runC binary.

00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:42.540
And when you run
the container, we

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:44.640
start up the container
in the user space

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:46.590
with our user space kernel.

00:17:46.590 --> 00:17:49.440
And we call this the sentry.

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:50.940
And then when the
container runs,

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:54.000
we use something called
a platform in order

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:56.550
to intercept the system
calls and reroute,

00:17:56.550 --> 00:17:59.460
direct those into the sentry.

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:02.920
And we use KVM and ptrace
as our two platforms

00:18:02.920 --> 00:18:07.290
that we provide as part of
the open source project.

00:18:07.290 --> 00:18:10.920
And then on top of
the sandbox itself, we

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:17.500
have the sandbox held inside
of a container, a normal host

00:18:17.500 --> 00:18:20.020
container which is using
seccomp and namespaces

00:18:20.020 --> 00:18:23.690
in order to further
isolate from the host.

00:18:23.690 --> 00:18:26.310
So as Yoshi mentioned, we
have two isolation layers.

00:18:26.310 --> 00:18:29.480
One is the sentry itself
in the user space kernel,

00:18:29.480 --> 00:18:31.970
and the second is
the host itself

00:18:31.970 --> 00:18:35.970
and the seccomp and namespaces
that the host provides.

00:18:35.970 --> 00:18:38.618
And so in order to get
control of the entire host,

00:18:38.618 --> 00:18:40.910
you would need to break
through both of those isolation

00:18:40.910 --> 00:18:43.230
boundaries.

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:45.930
In order to further
add isolation,

00:18:45.930 --> 00:18:47.670
we have something
called a gopher, which

00:18:47.670 --> 00:18:51.360
is a separate process
that gets run when

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:55.400
you run the runsc container.

00:18:55.400 --> 00:19:00.740
And that provides file
I/O. So the sentry

00:19:00.740 --> 00:19:03.980
itself does not have permission
to open files on the local file

00:19:03.980 --> 00:19:04.500
system.

00:19:04.500 --> 00:19:06.208
So even if you get
control of the sentry,

00:19:06.208 --> 00:19:08.360
you're not able to write
to the host file system

00:19:08.360 --> 00:19:11.270
and gain extra
privileges that way.

00:19:11.270 --> 00:19:13.700
You have to go through
a separate process that

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:18.980
is over 9P in order to
write files and deal

00:19:18.980 --> 00:19:23.780
with file I/O. This gives us
an extra layer, because there's

00:19:23.780 --> 00:19:28.100
a lot of different types
of attack services,

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:31.250
and gives us that extra
two levels of isolation

00:19:31.250 --> 00:19:32.450
for file I/O as well.

00:19:35.330 --> 00:19:38.130
So that's gVisor itself.

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:42.700
How does that fit into
GKE Sandbox and GKE?

00:19:42.700 --> 00:19:46.960
So with GKE, we provide a
managed control plane, an API

00:19:46.960 --> 00:19:51.830
server, and then each of
the nodes in the cluster

00:19:51.830 --> 00:19:54.520
is a GCE VM.

00:19:54.520 --> 00:19:56.530
And you can arrange
those in node pools.

00:19:56.530 --> 00:20:00.460
So a node pool is a group
of servers in your cluster

00:20:00.460 --> 00:20:02.440
or worker nodes in
your cluster that

00:20:02.440 --> 00:20:04.405
have the same configurations,
the same machine

00:20:04.405 --> 00:20:05.530
type, and things like that.

00:20:05.530 --> 00:20:09.113
And you can have multiple
node pools in your cluster.

00:20:09.113 --> 00:20:10.780
And so what GKE Sandbox
allows you to do

00:20:10.780 --> 00:20:13.810
is create node pools
that have gVisor enabled.

00:20:13.810 --> 00:20:16.330
So basically, add an extra
checkbox in the cloud console

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:19.150
or an extra flag on G
Cloud enables you to create

00:20:19.150 --> 00:20:24.460
a gVisor enabled node pool.

00:20:24.460 --> 00:20:27.430
And then when you
run your nodes,

00:20:27.430 --> 00:20:29.920
or you run your
workloads in the cluster

00:20:29.920 --> 00:20:34.530
and you specify gVisor as the
runtime, then you can run--

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:37.180
it will schedule and
run your workloads

00:20:37.180 --> 00:20:40.490
on your gVisor node pool.

00:20:40.490 --> 00:20:44.860
So let's drill down
into one of these nodes.

00:20:44.860 --> 00:20:49.010
Each of these nodes has an
agent, the Kubernetes agent,

00:20:49.010 --> 00:20:50.470
the Kubelet.

00:20:50.470 --> 00:20:53.500
And that Kubelet is responsible
for running the workloads

00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:57.040
on that VM or that worker node.

00:20:57.040 --> 00:20:58.990
And in order to
run containers that

00:20:58.990 --> 00:21:02.710
uses an extra runtime,
in this case, Containerd,

00:21:02.710 --> 00:21:05.290
which is high level
runtime, and that in turn

00:21:05.290 --> 00:21:07.990
uses OCI to use a low
level runtime in order

00:21:07.990 --> 00:21:09.280
to run containers.

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:12.960
So typically, this would
be done using runC.

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:18.390
But what we can do with our
gVisor enabled pod, or nodes,

00:21:18.390 --> 00:21:20.850
is to swap that out for gVisor.

00:21:20.850 --> 00:21:24.190
And this is enabled
because we're using the OCI

00:21:24.190 --> 00:21:25.440
as a standard interface.

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:32.350
So how do you use it from
a user's perspective?

00:21:32.350 --> 00:21:35.310
So from a user's perspective,
all you need to do

00:21:35.310 --> 00:21:41.270
is specify the runtime class
name as part of your pod spec.

00:21:41.270 --> 00:21:43.500
Kubernetes provides what's
called a RuntimeClass

00:21:43.500 --> 00:21:45.930
API, which is a
new API that allows

00:21:45.930 --> 00:21:48.570
you to find different
runtimes that you

00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:51.690
can use within your cluster.

00:21:51.690 --> 00:21:55.560
And GKE Sandbox provides
this automatically

00:21:55.560 --> 00:21:58.680
when you create a node pool
that is gVisor enabled.

00:21:58.680 --> 00:22:00.450
So you don't have to
worry, necessarily,

00:22:00.450 --> 00:22:02.910
about creating these runtime
classes and managing those.

00:22:02.910 --> 00:22:06.990
Those are more of a system
or a cluster administrator

00:22:06.990 --> 00:22:10.920
type of a role.

00:22:10.920 --> 00:22:14.490
But from a user's
perspective, or somebody

00:22:14.490 --> 00:22:16.140
wanting to run
workloads, all you

00:22:16.140 --> 00:22:19.830
need to do is put this
runtime class name of gVisor

00:22:19.830 --> 00:22:23.760
into your pod spec, and
then that will get run

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:26.670
in gVisor when you submit
that to the API server.

00:22:30.460 --> 00:22:32.560
So what kind of workloads
are good for gVisor

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:36.110
from a technical perspective?

00:22:36.110 --> 00:22:39.320
So gVisor works really well
for things like microservices,

00:22:39.320 --> 00:22:42.590
so things that are
very small and quick,

00:22:42.590 --> 00:22:46.970
and needs to come up and
down, and are highly scalable,

00:22:46.970 --> 00:22:48.860
as well as dense.

00:22:48.860 --> 00:22:52.940
So things that can take
advantage of the low overhead

00:22:52.940 --> 00:22:57.380
that gVisor provides work
really well in gVisor.

00:22:57.380 --> 00:23:00.170
So if you're running
microservices

00:23:00.170 --> 00:23:03.350
that make use of a lot
of third party code,

00:23:03.350 --> 00:23:05.120
perhaps you're using
something like NodeJS

00:23:05.120 --> 00:23:09.080
and you have a lot of external
libraries that you're using,

00:23:09.080 --> 00:23:12.350
or pretty much any other
type of language, running

00:23:12.350 --> 00:23:16.850
that in gVisor might be a way
of better ensuring that you have

00:23:16.850 --> 00:23:19.880
some extra isolation there.

00:23:19.880 --> 00:23:22.590
It's also really good for
SaaS and serverless workloads.

00:23:22.590 --> 00:23:27.410
So things that you're going
to be running code on a user's

00:23:27.410 --> 00:23:28.610
behalf.

00:23:28.610 --> 00:23:32.720
So people upload some code and
you run it on their behalf.

00:23:32.720 --> 00:23:36.710
Those type of things give you a
little bit of extra confidence

00:23:36.710 --> 00:23:39.170
that you're not exposing
your entire infrastructure

00:23:39.170 --> 00:23:40.775
to the code that gets uploaded.

00:23:43.960 --> 00:23:47.810
It's also really good for batch
and machine learning workloads.

00:23:47.810 --> 00:23:51.700
So gVisor, because it's
running as a user space kernel,

00:23:51.700 --> 00:23:53.530
it's running
directly on the CPU.

00:23:53.530 --> 00:23:57.460
And so CPU intensive workloads
work really well for it.

00:23:57.460 --> 00:24:01.210
It also is really
good for applications

00:24:01.210 --> 00:24:03.920
that run on this kind
of complex user input,

00:24:03.920 --> 00:24:08.170
so user input that comes
into the application and gets

00:24:08.170 --> 00:24:10.570
processed.

00:24:10.570 --> 00:24:13.816
Typically for things like
machine learning and batch,

00:24:13.816 --> 00:24:17.890
that data is user-submitted,
so things like uploaded images

00:24:17.890 --> 00:24:19.420
or uploaded data.

00:24:19.420 --> 00:24:24.120
And you typically
don't really want

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:27.100
to think about all the different
types of complex user input

00:24:27.100 --> 00:24:31.450
that could come in, and whether
well-crafted input could

00:24:31.450 --> 00:24:34.000
cause a security issue
with your application.

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:36.100
You'd like to be able to
just run it in a sandbox

00:24:36.100 --> 00:24:40.690
and not have to think that
much about the security

00:24:40.690 --> 00:24:41.832
implications.

00:24:44.550 --> 00:24:49.408
So let's take a look at the
software as a service use case.

00:24:49.408 --> 00:24:51.200
So I'm going to switch
over to a demo here.

00:24:54.440 --> 00:24:57.380
So what I've built
here is I've built

00:24:57.380 --> 00:25:00.670
an application
that is a WordPress

00:25:00.670 --> 00:25:02.050
software as a service.

00:25:02.050 --> 00:25:04.780
It's a very simple
service that allows

00:25:04.780 --> 00:25:08.290
me to create a
WordPress instance

00:25:08.290 --> 00:25:13.438
and run that WordPress
on behalf of my users.

00:25:13.438 --> 00:25:14.980
But what I want to
do is I want to be

00:25:14.980 --> 00:25:19.090
able to allow users to upload
or install any plugins that they

00:25:19.090 --> 00:25:22.690
want to as part of their
WordPress application.

00:25:22.690 --> 00:25:28.150
And those plugins can basically
contain arbitrary code, right?

00:25:28.150 --> 00:25:32.260
So these are plugins that are
available on the WordPress

00:25:32.260 --> 00:25:35.620
marketplace, and are
not really vetted

00:25:35.620 --> 00:25:39.350
by me in terms of their
security and that sort of thing.

00:25:39.350 --> 00:25:42.040
So what I want to do is
be able to allow people

00:25:42.040 --> 00:25:45.130
to create their own
plugins, to install plugins

00:25:45.130 --> 00:25:47.860
on the marketplace
without having

00:25:47.860 --> 00:25:51.500
to worry too much about
the security implications.

00:25:51.500 --> 00:25:53.890
So what I can do here is I
can create a new WordPress,

00:25:53.890 --> 00:25:56.650
and call that Ian's blog.

00:25:59.590 --> 00:26:01.520
And I can create
any number of these.

00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:02.890
I'll create Yoshi's blog too.

00:26:06.866 --> 00:26:08.330
And what this is
going to do, this

00:26:08.330 --> 00:26:10.650
is implemented as a simple
controller in Kubernetes.

00:26:10.650 --> 00:26:17.240
So this creates a CRD
that then gets kicked off

00:26:17.240 --> 00:26:22.040
and creates some pods
for me in my cluster.

00:26:22.040 --> 00:26:24.040
And so if I go over
here and I look

00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:28.090
at the state of
the cluster, and we

00:26:28.090 --> 00:26:29.950
see that I've got
to node pools here.

00:26:29.950 --> 00:26:32.380
And this is a
listing of the nodes.

00:26:32.380 --> 00:26:35.650
I first have my default
pool, which is just

00:26:35.650 --> 00:26:38.403
a non-gVisor enabled node pool.

00:26:38.403 --> 00:26:39.820
And then I have a
second node pool

00:26:39.820 --> 00:26:43.910
that is enabling gVisor here.

00:26:43.910 --> 00:26:46.450
And so if I go ahead
and get the pods that

00:26:46.450 --> 00:26:48.220
are running in my
cluster, I can see

00:26:48.220 --> 00:26:53.020
that I've started the
WordPress for my blog

00:26:53.020 --> 00:26:56.710
and I've started the
WordPress for Yoshi's blog.

00:26:56.710 --> 00:26:59.770
And we can see that the
WordPress blogs are running

00:26:59.770 --> 00:27:03.680
in our gVisor node pool.

00:27:03.680 --> 00:27:19.890
We can also see that
if we get the pod here,

00:27:19.890 --> 00:27:22.440
we can see that our
runtime class name here

00:27:22.440 --> 00:27:23.820
is specified as gVisor.

00:27:26.830 --> 00:27:31.060
So that means that it's running
our workload inside of gVisor

00:27:31.060 --> 00:27:32.466
itself.

00:27:32.466 --> 00:27:34.380
Great.

00:27:34.380 --> 00:27:36.820
All right, so our blogs
are up and running,

00:27:36.820 --> 00:27:38.790
so let's go ahead and
click on our blog here.

00:27:41.590 --> 00:27:44.680
And so what we'll have is we'll
have a brand new blog that's

00:27:44.680 --> 00:27:49.525
running inside of gVisor.

00:27:49.525 --> 00:27:51.400
And it's taking a little
bit to connect here.

00:28:05.910 --> 00:28:07.030
Let's try Yoshi's blog.

00:28:10.278 --> 00:28:12.600
All right.

00:28:12.600 --> 00:28:14.640
So we go ahead
and click English.

00:28:14.640 --> 00:28:17.110
Let's say the site's
title is Yoshi's blog.

00:28:21.110 --> 00:28:23.052
Create that.

00:28:23.052 --> 00:28:24.463
Hide this guy.

00:28:33.062 --> 00:28:33.770
Go ahead install.

00:28:39.020 --> 00:28:43.030
And log in to our new WordPress.

00:28:43.030 --> 00:28:45.960
And so this is a fully
functioning WordPress site.

00:28:45.960 --> 00:28:50.790
And so what we can do here is we
can go in and add new plugins.

00:28:50.790 --> 00:28:53.270
So we can install basically
any plugin we want here,

00:28:53.270 --> 00:29:00.260
like a classic editor, and
have that install and activate

00:29:00.260 --> 00:29:03.890
without having to worry too
much about what that actually

00:29:03.890 --> 00:29:09.340
contains in terms of the
code that's included in it.

00:29:09.340 --> 00:29:10.800
So what I'm going
to do here is I'm

00:29:10.800 --> 00:29:13.300
going to actually upload a new
plugin that I want to install

00:29:13.300 --> 00:29:15.690
that my friend Tim gave me.

00:29:15.690 --> 00:29:18.225
And this one's called Dirty COW.

00:29:18.225 --> 00:29:19.600
I'm not really
sure what's in it,

00:29:19.600 --> 00:29:21.975
but I'm just going to go ahead
and install it, because he

00:29:21.975 --> 00:29:24.400
said it's really cool.

00:29:24.400 --> 00:29:28.510
So if I install this guy,
it installed successfully.

00:29:28.510 --> 00:29:31.948
OK, so let's go ahead and
click on this Activate here.

00:29:31.948 --> 00:29:33.490
When we do that, it
kind of sits here

00:29:33.490 --> 00:29:34.820
and spins for a little while.

00:29:34.820 --> 00:29:36.195
I don't know if
you can see this,

00:29:36.195 --> 00:29:38.960
but it's sitting
here and spinning.

00:29:38.960 --> 00:29:41.680
But if we go into our
logs, we can see that--

00:29:44.266 --> 00:29:46.230
oh, it's not quite up yet.

00:29:46.230 --> 00:29:52.550
But what this is doing
in the background

00:29:52.550 --> 00:29:58.676
is it's actually running
the Dirty COW exploit as a--

00:29:58.676 --> 00:30:01.320
all right, here we can see it.

00:30:01.320 --> 00:30:03.080
So it's actually
running Dirty COW,

00:30:03.080 --> 00:30:07.700
trying to attempt to take over
my WordPress installation.

00:30:07.700 --> 00:30:10.220
So we can see here we're
running the Dirty COW exploit.

00:30:10.220 --> 00:30:13.490
And what Dirty COW does is
it uses madvise and ptrace,

00:30:13.490 --> 00:30:16.700
the system calls, and
running those in parallel

00:30:16.700 --> 00:30:18.800
in order to try to take
advantage of the system

00:30:18.800 --> 00:30:27.430
and take advantage of a
bug in the Linux kernel

00:30:27.430 --> 00:30:30.670
in order to actually take
control of the whole system.

00:30:30.670 --> 00:30:33.670
While this is kind
of an old bug,

00:30:33.670 --> 00:30:37.360
and that most Linux systems
are patched nowadays,

00:30:37.360 --> 00:30:41.500
this is just a good example
of the type of class of bugs

00:30:41.500 --> 00:30:50.300
that gVisor can prevent,
and that it helps mitigate.

00:30:50.300 --> 00:30:54.810
All right, so let's go back.

00:30:54.810 --> 00:30:57.220
Let's go back to
the slides, yeah.

00:30:57.220 --> 00:31:00.300
So next I want to
have Time come up

00:31:00.300 --> 00:31:04.538
and talk about machine learning
and the use cases around that,

00:31:04.538 --> 00:31:05.580
and what he's been doing.

00:31:08.270 --> 00:31:10.847
TIM KELTON: Thanks, Ian.

00:31:10.847 --> 00:31:11.680
You like my plug-in?

00:31:18.260 --> 00:31:21.650
So I don't know if any of you
have heard of Descartes Labs,

00:31:21.650 --> 00:31:27.470
but we're a startup that's a few
years old, about four years old

00:31:27.470 --> 00:31:28.280
now.

00:31:28.280 --> 00:31:31.220
And we're kind of focused on
the area of machine learning

00:31:31.220 --> 00:31:34.790
and on being able to
better observe the earth.

00:31:34.790 --> 00:31:39.170
And our background is, we're a
kind of stereotypical startup.

00:31:39.170 --> 00:31:41.940
We're up in the mountains
of Santa Fe, New Mexico.

00:31:41.940 --> 00:31:44.480
And we're there because
myself and the founding team

00:31:44.480 --> 00:31:48.200
were out of Los Alamos
National Laboratory.

00:31:48.200 --> 00:31:50.930
And there we were researchers
in high performance

00:31:50.930 --> 00:31:53.780
supercomputing, astrophysics.

00:31:53.780 --> 00:31:57.200
And among them, we did
research in things like machine

00:31:57.200 --> 00:32:02.400
learning and being able to apply
that to remote sensing data.

00:32:02.400 --> 00:32:04.500
And we do that because--

00:32:04.500 --> 00:32:06.650
and we started the
company to basically

00:32:06.650 --> 00:32:09.410
be able to understand
how changes happening all

00:32:09.410 --> 00:32:13.730
over the world will start
to impact our customers

00:32:13.730 --> 00:32:16.680
and their businesses,
their organizations,

00:32:16.680 --> 00:32:18.890
and even their supply chain.

00:32:18.890 --> 00:32:22.370
And I don't have to go
around and tell you there's

00:32:22.370 --> 00:32:24.440
a proliferation of sensors.

00:32:24.440 --> 00:32:27.470
All of you have phones,
and you know all about IoT

00:32:27.470 --> 00:32:29.870
and things like that.

00:32:29.870 --> 00:32:31.310
And that's on the ground.

00:32:31.310 --> 00:32:35.420
But we're having that same
parallel explosion in sensor

00:32:35.420 --> 00:32:37.940
technology happening in space.

00:32:37.940 --> 00:32:40.700
And so we're getting better
and better resolution,

00:32:40.700 --> 00:32:45.450
more and more modalities,
better temporal coverage.

00:32:45.450 --> 00:32:47.600
And it's just getting
cheaper and cheaper.

00:32:47.600 --> 00:32:50.150
These used to be half
billion dollar instruments

00:32:50.150 --> 00:32:51.440
that NASA put up.

00:32:51.440 --> 00:32:54.320
Now there's grad departments
that are making these.

00:32:54.320 --> 00:32:55.880
And there's startups
here in the Bay

00:32:55.880 --> 00:32:58.250
that are just launching them.

00:32:58.250 --> 00:33:00.360
So what that's going
to do is that's going

00:33:00.360 --> 00:33:03.190
to change how we can observe
and measure the earth.

00:33:03.190 --> 00:33:06.360
And it's fundamentally changing
a lot of the workflows.

00:33:06.360 --> 00:33:08.630
So a lot of the
datasets we leverage

00:33:08.630 --> 00:33:11.120
are really scientific in nature.

00:33:11.120 --> 00:33:15.170
They come from places like
NASA or European Space Agency.

00:33:15.170 --> 00:33:18.200
But more and more, we're
just seeing that hit

00:33:18.200 --> 00:33:20.030
on the commercial level.

00:33:20.030 --> 00:33:22.670
And this new type of
workflow is totally

00:33:22.670 --> 00:33:25.220
different than
historically what happened.

00:33:25.220 --> 00:33:28.280
Historically, you
would have an analyst

00:33:28.280 --> 00:33:31.370
and they would pull down an
image onto their desktop.

00:33:31.370 --> 00:33:33.230
Well, that worked
great when you had

00:33:33.230 --> 00:33:35.930
four scenes that were
captured once a month

00:33:35.930 --> 00:33:37.530
and things like that.

00:33:37.530 --> 00:33:43.130
But we've long passed when
the images and the data that

00:33:43.130 --> 00:33:45.980
was being captured was
humanly observable.

00:33:45.980 --> 00:33:48.500
And that's the type
of machine workflows

00:33:48.500 --> 00:33:52.420
that we're trying to leverage.

00:33:52.420 --> 00:33:58.810
So things like drought, and food
supply, and famine, and water--

00:33:58.810 --> 00:34:01.390
precious commodities that
go into building everything

00:34:01.390 --> 00:34:02.290
around us.

00:34:02.290 --> 00:34:05.050
Those are what impacts us.

00:34:05.050 --> 00:34:08.080
And so this is kind of this
new digital twin of the planet

00:34:08.080 --> 00:34:10.960
that we're trying to
measure and understand.

00:34:10.960 --> 00:34:16.560
And we have probably nearly
20 petabytes of data now

00:34:16.560 --> 00:34:20.010
that's stored up in the
cloud that we run and iterate

00:34:20.010 --> 00:34:21.159
on models.

00:34:21.159 --> 00:34:23.010
So that's great.

00:34:23.010 --> 00:34:27.429
We never could do that
before without the cloud.

00:34:27.429 --> 00:34:30.960
But it does create
some challenges.

00:34:30.960 --> 00:34:33.570
What we did was
originally make these APIs

00:34:33.570 --> 00:34:35.610
for our own machine
learning team

00:34:35.610 --> 00:34:41.429
so they could scale up and
test datasets, test models

00:34:41.429 --> 00:34:45.150
against these huge datasets.

00:34:45.150 --> 00:34:47.040
And when we run our--

00:34:47.040 --> 00:34:52.909
so we built our SaaS platform,
obviously, on the cloud.

00:34:52.909 --> 00:34:57.290
And often, it's not
just those datasets

00:34:57.290 --> 00:34:59.060
that our customers want to use.

00:34:59.060 --> 00:35:01.580
They want to build
their own models.

00:35:01.580 --> 00:35:05.570
They want to often use one of
their pieces of data to get

00:35:05.570 --> 00:35:07.740
new insights for their company.

00:35:07.740 --> 00:35:10.880
So I thought I'd show
you some examples.

00:35:10.880 --> 00:35:13.810
Warning, this is going
to be lots of pictures.

00:35:13.810 --> 00:35:15.260
I hope that's OK.

00:35:15.260 --> 00:35:16.970
It's Thursday.

00:35:16.970 --> 00:35:20.340
So this is a solar
facility in Morocco.

00:35:20.340 --> 00:35:24.560
It's pretty amazing to look at.

00:35:24.560 --> 00:35:27.020
But often, it's
temporally how things

00:35:27.020 --> 00:35:29.660
change that give
us those insights,

00:35:29.660 --> 00:35:32.990
and we need to be
able to understand.

00:35:32.990 --> 00:35:35.330
So obviously, this is the
construction and the building

00:35:35.330 --> 00:35:37.530
of that facility.

00:35:37.530 --> 00:35:41.870
Another example is
showing patterns of life.

00:35:41.870 --> 00:35:45.290
Often we need to fuse
together different sensors,

00:35:45.290 --> 00:35:47.540
and not just one.

00:35:47.540 --> 00:35:50.540
So in the top row is just
normal red, green, and blue,

00:35:50.540 --> 00:35:52.910
like all of us as
humans can see.

00:35:52.910 --> 00:35:58.540
But the bottom row is
fusing together infrared.

00:35:58.540 --> 00:36:00.880
And you'll notice on the
far bottom right, one

00:36:00.880 --> 00:36:04.900
of the big things you can't see
with just human visible light

00:36:04.900 --> 00:36:08.260
is that thermal signature.

00:36:08.260 --> 00:36:12.750
We do this for all
different types of examples.

00:36:12.750 --> 00:36:15.940
We're starting to now
run a wildfire detection

00:36:15.940 --> 00:36:20.050
algorithm that will run over the
entire western half of the US.

00:36:20.050 --> 00:36:23.170
We don't know exactly where
a wildfire might happen,

00:36:23.170 --> 00:36:25.450
but being able to detect
those thermal signatures

00:36:25.450 --> 00:36:28.210
is important.

00:36:28.210 --> 00:36:29.690
This is another example.

00:36:29.690 --> 00:36:33.700
This is more in the computer
vision and types of things

00:36:33.700 --> 00:36:38.230
that you would use TensorFlow
or those types of tools.

00:36:38.230 --> 00:36:43.960
This is a model that can detect
buildings and structures.

00:36:43.960 --> 00:36:48.820
And last fall, there
were some hurricanes that

00:36:48.820 --> 00:36:51.470
were heading toward Florida.

00:36:51.470 --> 00:36:54.310
And so we were saying,
well, could we actually

00:36:54.310 --> 00:36:56.720
scale this model across
the whole state of Florida?

00:36:56.720 --> 00:36:59.590
So that took us a couple hours.

00:36:59.590 --> 00:37:04.230
And that's across hundreds
of terabytes of data.

00:37:04.230 --> 00:37:06.980
So why might you also
want to use or know

00:37:06.980 --> 00:37:11.210
about where all the
buildings are being produced?

00:37:11.210 --> 00:37:14.670
Well, construction
takes raw materials.

00:37:14.670 --> 00:37:18.470
And those correlate into
commodities and demand

00:37:18.470 --> 00:37:19.530
structures.

00:37:19.530 --> 00:37:21.580
So in this case, steel.

00:37:21.580 --> 00:37:24.500
And again, this is just things
that weren't really visible

00:37:24.500 --> 00:37:27.650
or weren't very easy to measure
without tremendous amounts

00:37:27.650 --> 00:37:30.500
of computing power and
that massive data that's

00:37:30.500 --> 00:37:32.440
being collected.

00:37:32.440 --> 00:37:35.360
Water, already talked about
that a bit once before.

00:37:35.360 --> 00:37:36.790
This is a different modality.

00:37:36.790 --> 00:37:39.380
We're sending a radar
signal down to the earth.

00:37:39.380 --> 00:37:41.300
It bounces off the
earth's surface.

00:37:41.300 --> 00:37:43.610
It gives us almost a
height measurement.

00:37:43.610 --> 00:37:48.890
You can see heights in
reservoirs and areas like that.

00:37:48.890 --> 00:37:51.560
You think about how many
areas water is an input

00:37:51.560 --> 00:37:52.770
to the pattern of life.

00:37:52.770 --> 00:37:55.100
So that's everything
from food and food supply

00:37:55.100 --> 00:37:58.410
and the types of
models we build there.

00:37:58.410 --> 00:38:00.410
And those of us
that live in places

00:38:00.410 --> 00:38:06.200
like the West, western half of
the US, New Mexico, California,

00:38:06.200 --> 00:38:10.700
we know how volatile
a water supply can be.

00:38:10.700 --> 00:38:13.640
Once you know about buildings,
you might care about trees,

00:38:13.640 --> 00:38:16.100
or encroachment of trees.

00:38:16.100 --> 00:38:18.020
Looking down and
being able to identify

00:38:18.020 --> 00:38:22.580
a tree versus vegetation
is actually really hard.

00:38:22.580 --> 00:38:25.670
They both look like
vegetation that's growing.

00:38:25.670 --> 00:38:27.240
There's photosynthesis
occurring.

00:38:27.240 --> 00:38:29.148
You can see both
of them the same.

00:38:29.148 --> 00:38:31.190
This is the place where
we are able to take LIDAR

00:38:31.190 --> 00:38:35.810
to train and see the height, and
then be able to differentiate

00:38:35.810 --> 00:38:40.570
and apply this to places where
we didn't have LIDAR coverage,

00:38:40.570 --> 00:38:43.580
and basically run this
over the whole globe.

00:38:43.580 --> 00:38:46.280
And again, you think
about last summer

00:38:46.280 --> 00:38:49.910
out here in California, look at
some of the devastating fires.

00:38:49.910 --> 00:38:52.370
We were able to scale
and run this model

00:38:52.370 --> 00:38:57.030
and map out every single tree in
the entire state of California.

00:38:57.030 --> 00:39:00.470
So that's just-- it took
lots and lots of computing

00:39:00.470 --> 00:39:02.900
horsepower and lots of storage.

00:39:02.900 --> 00:39:04.940
But these are just a
couple quick examples.

00:39:04.940 --> 00:39:06.890
And I really show
them just to point out

00:39:06.890 --> 00:39:11.240
how wide the use cases are,
how varying the datasets are.

00:39:11.240 --> 00:39:15.210
Now think how different the
code and the models have to be.

00:39:15.210 --> 00:39:18.200
There's not just a magic
model and there's not

00:39:18.200 --> 00:39:20.590
a magic dataset.

00:39:20.590 --> 00:39:25.210
So when our customers are
building these types of models,

00:39:25.210 --> 00:39:27.800
what are they coming
to us and asking for?

00:39:27.800 --> 00:39:29.800
Well, they care deeply
about the physical world.

00:39:29.800 --> 00:39:32.800
A lot of them are
Fortune 500 companies.

00:39:32.800 --> 00:39:35.900
They care about
physical infrastructure.

00:39:35.900 --> 00:39:38.260
But a lot of them are very
concerned about security

00:39:38.260 --> 00:39:39.340
as well.

00:39:39.340 --> 00:39:41.600
We all are.

00:39:41.600 --> 00:39:44.380
And often, they're
wanting to train a model,

00:39:44.380 --> 00:39:48.820
so make their own intellectual
property off their data, which

00:39:48.820 --> 00:39:51.252
is their intellectual property.

00:39:51.252 --> 00:39:52.960
And they want to know
that their model is

00:39:52.960 --> 00:39:56.780
isolated from other customers,
and it's safe and secure.

00:39:56.780 --> 00:40:00.160
And so we want to enable them
to be able to build and train

00:40:00.160 --> 00:40:03.490
and test those models about
all of our petabytes of data--

00:40:03.490 --> 00:40:07.450
so it kind of has to
live next to the data--

00:40:07.450 --> 00:40:09.280
and know that the
security isolation

00:40:09.280 --> 00:40:13.810
isn't going to slow down or
cause delays in the training.

00:40:13.810 --> 00:40:15.550
And then they want
to have confidence

00:40:15.550 --> 00:40:17.050
that they'll be able to--

00:40:17.050 --> 00:40:19.000
that other users of
the system aren't

00:40:19.000 --> 00:40:21.190
going to be able to
access their data

00:40:21.190 --> 00:40:23.350
or their intellectual property.

00:40:23.350 --> 00:40:27.290
So what challenges
do we have when

00:40:27.290 --> 00:40:30.560
trying to build that
type of a platform?

00:40:30.560 --> 00:40:34.760
Well, each model is
executing someone else--

00:40:34.760 --> 00:40:38.050
one of our user's
machine learning code.

00:40:38.050 --> 00:40:43.750
So we need to be able to execute
that securely and at scale.

00:40:43.750 --> 00:40:47.800
Each model has very
different requirements.

00:40:47.800 --> 00:40:51.190
Some of these models
are very CPU intensive.

00:40:51.190 --> 00:40:53.440
Others are very
memory intensive.

00:40:53.440 --> 00:40:57.040
Maybe some of them
need to use a GPU.

00:40:57.040 --> 00:40:59.020
These are pretty big in scale.

00:40:59.020 --> 00:41:01.060
I'll come in most
mornings and I don't

00:41:01.060 --> 00:41:05.770
see 10 or 20 cores running
on our [? graphonograph. ?]

00:41:05.770 --> 00:41:09.640
I see tens of thousands
of CPU cores that

00:41:09.640 --> 00:41:11.860
are running, that somebody
is going out and building

00:41:11.860 --> 00:41:15.280
models or testing models
against those data sets.

00:41:15.280 --> 00:41:19.360
So it needs to scale
and be efficient.

00:41:19.360 --> 00:41:22.120
And we thought
originally of doing this

00:41:22.120 --> 00:41:25.090
through isolating
with GKE node pools

00:41:25.090 --> 00:41:27.700
and making one node
pool per customer.

00:41:27.700 --> 00:41:30.570
But that runs into
a lot of limitations

00:41:30.570 --> 00:41:32.890
once you start getting
a few customers.

00:41:32.890 --> 00:41:36.310
We thought of using a
cluster, a whole Kubernetes

00:41:36.310 --> 00:41:38.260
cluster per customer.

00:41:38.260 --> 00:41:40.758
That also runs into
a lot of limitations.

00:41:40.758 --> 00:41:42.550
And we wanted to do
this-- we're a startup.

00:41:42.550 --> 00:41:44.770
We don't have just
infinite resources.

00:41:44.770 --> 00:41:46.480
Most of you don't either.

00:41:46.480 --> 00:41:49.090
And so we're trying to do this
as efficiently as possible

00:41:49.090 --> 00:41:53.290
from a cost perspective and
still get that security.

00:41:53.290 --> 00:41:56.800
So we wanted to leverage
Kubernetes scheduler

00:41:56.800 --> 00:41:59.840
to get that really
tight bin packing.

00:41:59.840 --> 00:42:06.230
So on GCP, there's a lot of
great tools for data and things

00:42:06.230 --> 00:42:07.370
like data exfiltration.

00:42:07.370 --> 00:42:09.920
We leverage things like
VPC service controls

00:42:09.920 --> 00:42:12.530
to prevent data exfiltration.

00:42:12.530 --> 00:42:15.110
But for models and
model execution,

00:42:15.110 --> 00:42:17.250
we need to execute
that user's code.

00:42:17.250 --> 00:42:19.490
So the first thing
we do is we carve out

00:42:19.490 --> 00:42:22.760
a user namespace for each user.

00:42:22.760 --> 00:42:28.420
And then we restrict
what they can talk to.

00:42:28.420 --> 00:42:31.960
They cannot talk to the
Kubernetes control plane under

00:42:31.960 --> 00:42:33.130
the hood.

00:42:33.130 --> 00:42:36.820
They can only talk to
our Descartes Labs APIs.

00:42:36.820 --> 00:42:40.180
So that's a really
important separation.

00:42:40.180 --> 00:42:44.110
And then our Descartes Labs APIs
are talking to the Kubernetes

00:42:44.110 --> 00:42:45.050
control plane.

00:42:45.050 --> 00:42:48.830
And that's how we're scaling
up and down the workloads.

00:42:48.830 --> 00:42:52.280
And then for our
Descartes Labs APIs

00:42:52.280 --> 00:42:54.800
to get visibility
into what's going on,

00:42:54.800 --> 00:42:56.750
to be able to do things
like rate limiting

00:42:56.750 --> 00:43:01.220
or security telemetry, that's
all handled through Istio.

00:43:01.220 --> 00:43:03.830
Our user workloads,
as Ian just showed,

00:43:03.830 --> 00:43:07.970
those go in a GKE
Sandbox node pool.

00:43:07.970 --> 00:43:10.370
And so as it's been
mentioned, that

00:43:10.370 --> 00:43:12.080
improves a lot with security.

00:43:12.080 --> 00:43:14.840
It limits assist
calls to the kernel.

00:43:14.840 --> 00:43:17.210
It separates each user.

00:43:17.210 --> 00:43:22.310
And it's a lot more efficient
than making a node pool

00:43:22.310 --> 00:43:24.630
for every single customer.

00:43:24.630 --> 00:43:29.670
For us, we get much
improved on the cost side.

00:43:29.670 --> 00:43:33.680
We run most of our workloads
on things like preemptable VMs.

00:43:33.680 --> 00:43:36.240
So that's critical to be
able to use that and make

00:43:36.240 --> 00:43:39.420
that efficient in
terms of pricing.

00:43:39.420 --> 00:43:42.060
And we get the tighter bin
packing of those workloads

00:43:42.060 --> 00:43:44.430
and then still
improve isolation.

00:43:44.430 --> 00:43:49.290
So how did we
enable GKE Sandbox?

00:43:49.290 --> 00:43:53.580
As was mentioned, you
basically set the sandbox type

00:43:53.580 --> 00:43:59.730
to gVisor when you do
a node pool creation.

00:43:59.730 --> 00:44:03.300
And what's really great was
we didn't change our code.

00:44:03.300 --> 00:44:06.090
We didn't have to have our
users change their code

00:44:06.090 --> 00:44:08.280
to get this degree of isolation.

00:44:08.280 --> 00:44:10.410
And we could run this
on an existing cluster.

00:44:10.410 --> 00:44:13.680
We simply just made
a new node pool.

00:44:13.680 --> 00:44:16.320
So it's very easy to get
this started and just start

00:44:16.320 --> 00:44:19.330
testing your workloads
and see how they do.

00:44:19.330 --> 00:44:20.820
So the next question
you might say

00:44:20.820 --> 00:44:23.310
is, well, how did
it actually perform

00:44:23.310 --> 00:44:26.840
for these types of machine
learning workloads?

00:44:26.840 --> 00:44:29.730
The basic test case--

00:44:29.730 --> 00:44:31.920
we've only tested this
on a few workloads.

00:44:31.920 --> 00:44:35.790
But the startup cost is
around about a second.

00:44:35.790 --> 00:44:40.590
And we're running models
in minutes to hours today.

00:44:40.590 --> 00:44:43.200
So we can live with a second.

00:44:43.200 --> 00:44:48.780
And then those rough models
are about 1% difference

00:44:48.780 --> 00:44:50.530
in terms of overall performance.

00:44:50.530 --> 00:44:53.190
So to be honest, I would
say that's within the margin

00:44:53.190 --> 00:44:55.690
of error in our measurements.

00:44:55.690 --> 00:44:58.680
So that's more than adequate.

00:44:58.680 --> 00:45:01.310
And so this is now
available, and we're

00:45:01.310 --> 00:45:03.950
making this available to
all of our customers today.

00:45:03.950 --> 00:45:06.748
With that, I'll
hand it to Yoshi.

00:45:06.748 --> 00:45:08.290
YOSHI TAMURA: Thank
you so much, Tim.

00:45:08.290 --> 00:45:10.510
It's great to see this is
not like a demonstration.

00:45:10.510 --> 00:45:13.270
It's running in your real,
real system, seeing the value.

00:45:13.270 --> 00:45:15.030
So as Tim quickly
touched, I'm going

00:45:15.030 --> 00:45:18.405
to dig a little bit
deeper on the performance

00:45:18.405 --> 00:45:20.700
to set the tone correct.

00:45:20.700 --> 00:45:23.260
GKE Sandbox is about
boosting your performance.

00:45:23.260 --> 00:45:25.330
It's all about
providing isolation.

00:45:25.330 --> 00:45:27.090
And providing that
sort of isolation

00:45:27.090 --> 00:45:28.570
and [? national ?]
defense, we're

00:45:28.570 --> 00:45:30.940
going to take some cycles
from your workflows

00:45:30.940 --> 00:45:33.760
and give back this
defense with ease,

00:45:33.760 --> 00:45:35.830
and which will lead
to the cost efficiency

00:45:35.830 --> 00:45:39.810
that Tim just described.

00:45:39.810 --> 00:45:43.440
So for machine learning
workloads, like Tim introduced,

00:45:43.440 --> 00:45:46.200
yes, these are some--

00:45:46.200 --> 00:45:49.560
this is a kind of data
from the machine learning

00:45:49.560 --> 00:45:52.650
inference workflows
or a prediction.

00:45:52.650 --> 00:45:57.900
And these types of CPU and
memory oriented workloads,

00:45:57.900 --> 00:46:00.210
the cycles that we need
to provide to give you

00:46:00.210 --> 00:46:02.520
back the isolation
that Tim talked about,

00:46:02.520 --> 00:46:06.120
are roughly about 2% to
3% of [? overhead. ?]

00:46:06.120 --> 00:46:08.120
So if you're wondering,
like the workloads,

00:46:08.120 --> 00:46:10.860
like you have similar
workloads like Tim's, this

00:46:10.860 --> 00:46:15.030
should be a very good fit for
you, because with just only 2%

00:46:15.030 --> 00:46:19.720
of a cycle, you get these
kind of benefit upfront.

00:46:19.720 --> 00:46:23.770
Now, workloads like Redis,
I think a lot of you

00:46:23.770 --> 00:46:24.830
may know about Redis.

00:46:24.830 --> 00:46:27.760
It's in-memory data store,
one of the most popular

00:46:27.760 --> 00:46:29.830
staple workloads on GKE.

00:46:29.830 --> 00:46:35.410
These type of workflows need
20% to 30% of cycles from you

00:46:35.410 --> 00:46:37.120
to return to benefit.

00:46:37.120 --> 00:46:39.970
But if you have SaaS
and multi-tenancy,

00:46:39.970 --> 00:46:42.910
for example, if you have Redis
instance for each customer

00:46:42.910 --> 00:46:44.890
where today you create
a different cluster,

00:46:44.890 --> 00:46:48.100
or nodes even, then GKE
Sandbox should still

00:46:48.100 --> 00:46:51.970
be able to help you greatly to
provide you this bin packing

00:46:51.970 --> 00:46:55.860
capability with ease.

00:46:55.860 --> 00:46:59.370
Lastly, I'm going to
show about the FFmpeg

00:46:59.370 --> 00:47:01.040
and then Wordpress use case.

00:47:01.040 --> 00:47:04.260
So FFmpeg is a well-known
transcoding open source

00:47:04.260 --> 00:47:04.830
program.

00:47:04.830 --> 00:47:07.690
And Wordpress, as
Ian already showed,

00:47:07.690 --> 00:47:10.980
is a blog and web
hosting platform.

00:47:10.980 --> 00:47:15.030
They are actually both network
and file oriented workflows.

00:47:15.030 --> 00:47:18.240
However, the way they do the
I/O are actually different.

00:47:18.240 --> 00:47:22.620
Compared to WordPress, which
will issue I/O one by one,

00:47:22.620 --> 00:47:25.950
FFmpeg actually gathers
I/O very nice way,

00:47:25.950 --> 00:47:28.080
so that GKE Sandbox
doesn't have to take

00:47:28.080 --> 00:47:31.220
too many cycles from you.

00:47:31.220 --> 00:47:36.120
It's only about 5% to actually--

00:47:36.120 --> 00:47:39.270
5% is pretty much
the cycles that we

00:47:39.270 --> 00:47:44.400
need to give you back
this isolation, which will

00:47:44.400 --> 00:47:46.030
lead to the cost efficiency.

00:47:46.030 --> 00:47:48.300
So if you're
wondering, OK, I'm now

00:47:48.300 --> 00:47:50.420
interested in a GKE
Sandbox, but which workflows

00:47:50.420 --> 00:47:53.370
will be the great fit,
actually, this few [? higher ?]

00:47:53.370 --> 00:47:56.010
number I/Os should be
a very good guideline

00:47:56.010 --> 00:48:01.390
to find workflows that will be
very, very beneficial by using

00:48:01.390 --> 00:48:01.950
GKE Sandbox.

00:48:05.130 --> 00:48:07.200
Just wrapping up then.

00:48:07.200 --> 00:48:10.170
So we introduced GKE Sandbox.

00:48:10.170 --> 00:48:13.470
This will be publicly
available in late April.

00:48:13.470 --> 00:48:17.090
All the things are queued
up waiting for after Next

00:48:17.090 --> 00:48:19.780
to unlock everything.

00:48:19.780 --> 00:48:23.160
GKE Sandbox will provide defense
in-depth to your Kubernetes pod

00:48:23.160 --> 00:48:24.180
with ease.

00:48:24.180 --> 00:48:25.650
No application changes.

00:48:25.650 --> 00:48:27.450
Like two lines,
which Tim showed,

00:48:27.450 --> 00:48:29.840
is exactly what you have to do.

00:48:29.840 --> 00:48:31.450
It will be great for
a team interested

00:48:31.450 --> 00:48:34.380
in SaaS and multi-tenancy.

00:48:34.380 --> 00:48:37.950
And it's based on open source
gVisor, which Ian talked about.

00:48:37.950 --> 00:48:41.700
And we put a lot of effort into
providing better experience

00:48:41.700 --> 00:48:42.510
as product.

00:48:42.510 --> 00:48:45.810
All you have to do is to just,
in turn, create a node pool,

00:48:45.810 --> 00:48:47.360
and turn it on in your pod spec.

00:48:47.360 --> 00:48:49.940
Rest of the things will
happen behind the scene.

00:48:49.940 --> 00:48:52.850
And we also added, actually,
a lot of optimization

00:48:52.850 --> 00:48:53.740
around performance.

00:48:53.740 --> 00:48:55.770
So if you're wondering
which workloads

00:48:55.770 --> 00:49:00.390
to use for GKE Sandbox,
please look at the numbers

00:49:00.390 --> 00:49:02.013
that I kind of shared today.

00:49:02.013 --> 00:49:03.180
That's [? pretty much ?] it.

00:49:03.180 --> 00:49:04.597
Thank you very
much for listening.

